Check and bind a storage buffer by the size its borrower asked for

The frame pool hands out the smallest idle block that is at least the
request and at most max(request, 64 MiB) larger. dispatch() then checked
that block's capacity (b->bytes) against maxStorageBufferRange and bound the
whole block. A plane that fits the limit could therefore fail with "buffer
exceeds maxStorageBufferRange" after a reuse, only because the pool lent it
a bigger block. On Adreno 7xx the limit is 128 MiB, about the size of the
planes the app sizes against it (DeviceProfile.maxPixels), so this is the
case that matters.

This checks and binds requested_bytes, which allocate() already records for
every hand-out (fresh, reused, persistent). Persistent and promoted buffers
have requested_bytes == bytes, so nothing changes for them. No kernel reads
a buffer's length() (checked: grep over shaders/vulkan), every kernel bounds
itself by its meta counts, so the narrower range changes nothing a shader
can see and the output is byte-identical.

--- a/engine/src/gpu/vulkan_gpu.cpp
+++ b/engine/src/gpu/vulkan_gpu.cpp
@@ -650,11 +650,18 @@
                 b = inline_buffers.back().get();
             }
             if (!b) { error = std::string(kernel) + ": empty argument " + std::to_string(i); return false; }
-            if (b->bytes > storage_range_limit_) {
+            // Android: check and bind what the borrower asked for, not the pool
+            // block it was handed. A reused block can be up to max(request,
+            // 64 MiB) larger, so a plane that fits the limit failed the check
+            // after a reuse. Kernels bound themselves by their meta counts and
+            // none reads a buffer's length(), so the narrower range changes
+            // nothing they can see.
+            const size_t range = b->requested_bytes ? b->requested_bytes : b->bytes;
+            if (range > storage_range_limit_) {
                 error = std::string(kernel) + ": buffer exceeds maxStorageBufferRange";
                 return false;
             }
-            infos[i] = {b->handle, 0, VkDeviceSize(b->bytes)};
+            infos[i] = {b->handle, 0, VkDeviceSize(range)};
         }
 
         std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(dispatch_mutex_);
